WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:02.640
- [Presenter] The first
solar panels were invented

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:04.770
in America in 1954,

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:06.990
and yet, it's been China
that's been better able

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:08.910
to capitalize on the technologies.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:10.650
- China was one of the first countries

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:14.430
to realize the potential of solar panels.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:15.750
China actually took it

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.570
and really commercialized
it at a large scale.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:20.970
- [Presenter] Now, China controls over 80%

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:23.160
of the global solar panel supply chain.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.420
While the United States,

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:27.120
manufactures virtually none
of the required components

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:28.860
for solar panel production.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:30.720
So to effectively compete with China

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.610
in solar panel manufacturing,

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:35.970
the US is prioritizing
building up its supply chain,

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:37.683
basically, from scratch.

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:41.370
Today, the US has less than half

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.560
of the solar capacity that China does,

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:46.320
and of the solar panels that
are installed in the US,

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.060
nearly four out of five of them

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:49.350
are from Chinese companies.

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:50.880
- China has such a headstart

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:53.400
when it comes to solar
panel manufacturing,

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.370
it's because they dominate the
entire global supply chain.

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:58.020
- [Presenter] That's
because in recent years,

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.330
China has spent almost 10 times as much

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:03.900
on solar manufacturing than
the US and the EU combined.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:07.410
And of the world's top 10
largest solar manufacturers,

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:10.440
seven are Chinese, only one is American.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.150
That stark difference plays out

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:13.650
in each country's energy mix.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:17.250
According to the EIA,
solar energy makes up 12%

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:20.190
of China's total electricity
generation capacity,

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:22.950
yet, it only makes up about 3.4%

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:25.920
of the US' total electricity
generation capacity.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.890
- It was really this idea that
they were just going to shoot

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:33.330
for the moon and go big, and
that has huge implications

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:36.270
and dragged frankly, the rest of the world

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:40.683
into this clean energy as a
real option, not a play thing.

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:42.780
- [Presenter] But one key area

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:45.870
where the US has an
advantage is silica sand,

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:48.600
the primary mineral used
for solar generation.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.530
It exports about $470
million worth of the mineral,

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:56.040
and China is the world's
largest importer of silica sand,

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.170
importing it mainly
from the United States,

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:00.180
Australia, and Indonesia.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:03.180
To make a panel, silica
sand needs to be refined

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:06.390
to create purified silicon,
which is shaped into ingots

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:08.370
and sliced into thin wafers.

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:11.520
The wafers are cut into
cells and panel manufacturers

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:13.260
then assemble the cells in a frame

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:16.560
and add circuitry to create
a finished module or panel.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:19.560
But even though the United
States has the raw materials,

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:23.070
it has no manufacturers
of silicon wafers or cells

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:24.813
used for solar panel production.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.340
- Manufacturing isn't just
some Lego play, right?

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:33.240
It's about figuring out how
to do something quicker,

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:36.090
more efficiently, better than others.

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:38.340
- [Presenter] Because China's
able to produce solar panels

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:41.160
at a significantly lower
cost in global markets,

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:44.100
the few US solar
manufacturers that do exist

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:47.490
heavily rely on importing
components exclusive to China

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:49.410
and then assembling them domestically.

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:52.170
- The reason China has been able

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:56.670
to bring down costs
actually and sustain quality

00:02:56.670 --> 00:03:01.110
of solar panels, they decided
that they were gonna build

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:04.110
bigger plants, much bigger in size

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:06.990
than the United States
had in the early days.

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:10.290
They incorporated new
manufacturing approaches

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:14.010
including robotics, and
they were so much larger

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:17.610
with so much less human interaction

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:19.530
that they were able to scale up.

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:23.160
- And this is actually China's real skill,

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.730
how to make things and make
it commercially viable,

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:30.840
and then cheap and accessible.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:32.100
- The Inflation Reduction Act

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.740
is also the most
significant investment ever

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:36.030
in climate change.

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:37.380
- [Presenter] The Inflation Reduction Act

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:40.920
passed in August, 2022, hopes
to accelerate the transition

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:42.780
to a clean energy economy.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:45.510
For solar manufacturing,
that means incentives

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:48.090
for boosting panel
installations and tax credits

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:51.450
for manufacturing solar
components in the United States.

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:55.470
- We, in the US, have never
had really industrial policy

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:57.690
to support that kind of
manufacturing, right?

00:03:57.690 --> 00:03:59.610
It's only now with the
Inflation Reduction Act

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:02.820
that we have the kind of
domestic industrial policy

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:04.980
that will support solar manufacturing

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:06.990
and storage manufacturing here in the US.

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:10.470
- Already, the Inflation
Reduction Act has led First Solar,

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:12.780
the biggest solar panel maker in the US

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:15.600
to spend $1.2 billion to increase

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:19.140
its US manufacturing capacity by 75%.

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:21.930
- If we're going to continue
to scale up the industry

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:23.760
in order to address the climate crisis,

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:25.560
we need to bring more of
that manufacturing home,

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:26.580
and we're doing it.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:29.130
- [Presenter] But because
China is ahead by so much,

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:30.780
there is debate whether the US

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:32.910
should try to invest money in the sector.

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:34.650
- The United States may not be able

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:38.340
to actually really catch up
with China as much as that.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:41.640
It may just be able to
onshore some of the skills,

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:43.380
so that it can do it itself.

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:48.380
- I remind people that energy
is used in everything we do,

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:53.160
our electricity, our
transportation, our infrastructure,

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:57.492
our communications, our
agriculture, everything.

00:04:57.492 --> 00:05:00.075
(gentle music)

